// facet — platform-free UI description + the `@facet { }` builder context.
//
// A `@facet { ... }` block (the compiler's contextual-builder DSL) desugars
// to `facet::Builder::new()` + `.add(item)` + `.finish()`. Bare leaf names
// resolve against this package (`label` -> `facet::label`), a bare container
// (`vstack { ... }`) calls the container fn, and modifiers follow the
// flex_layout line rule: same line = fluent chain (`take this -> Node`),
// own leading-dot line = in-place `set_*`.
//
// The result is a `Node` tree — pure data, no native types. `mount` turns it
// into a `flex_layout` node tree through a per-platform `Renderer` vtable
// (one op per kind). Containers, spacing, and every layout prop are applied
// HERE, write-once; widget kinds dispatch through the vtable. After every
// widget-producing op, `mount` calls `set_identity` so agent id + role are
// pinned once, not re-remembered inside each op. A platform package
// (facet_appkit, ...) supplies those fns over its typed binding, then lays
// the flex tree out and applies the frames.
//
// The leaf/container value IS a `Node` (no wrapper): the DSL's `add` moves a
// whole owned value into the accumulator, and `finish`/containers hand the
// accumulated `Vec` back out by destructuring the `Builder` — a zero-copy
// whole-value move.

import "stdlib/vec" as vec;
import "stdlib/text" as text;
import "stdlib/option" as option;
import "stdlib/box" as box;
import "stdlib/thread" as thread;
import "stdlib/future" as future;
import "stdlib/executor" as executor;
import "stdlib/net" as net;
import "flex_layout/flex_layout" as flex;

// ---- the component contract ---------------------------------------------
// A component is a plain struct: its FIELDS are the state, its handlers are
// methods in the INHERENT impl, and this interface is the checked conformance
// block (`impl Foo: facet::Component`) holding `build`. Interface-impl blocks
// are closed (E0503/E0504 typo interlock), so `build` is verified against this
// declaration both ways.
//
// `build` returns the PORTABLE DESCRIPTION — a facet `Node` tree, pure data,
// no platform types — and is called ONCE. The backend mounts that description
// into native views and RETAINS the tree; from then on the tree is live and
// you edit it IN PLACE.
//
// facet is NOT reactive. There is no re-render and no diff: `build` is never
// called again, so there is no fresh tree to reconcile against the old one. You
// already hold the element's key, so there is no delta to rediscover. (Contrast
// React/Flutter: mutate state, rebuild, diff the tree, patch. facet skips all of
// that — it addresses the exact element and sets it.)
//
// Two ways to update, never a third:
//   1. by key    — `facet::find(cp, key).set_text(...)` resolves the one keyed
//                  element in this component's live subtree and mutates it (see
//                  `find`). `cp` is the component's own address; a handler
//                  receives it as its `ctx`.
//   2. by method — call a method on the component; internally it updates its own
//                  UI by key. Same mechanism, wrapped behind a named action.
//
// State lives in the struct's fields — the component is retained, so they persist
// for the life of the tree. A handler writes a field, then pushes that value to
// the element that shows it. Nothing is recomputed.
//
// The React trap to avoid: re-calling `build` when state changes, holding the
// tree to "render it top-down each frame," or minting a fresh child inside a
// parent's `build` on every call. `build` runs ONCE and returns already-owned
// children; after that, you `find` and mutate.
interface Component {
    // `build` takes `ref this` so a component can bind its own handlers directly
    // — `button(...).on_click(this.method)` — where the bound method's receiver
    // is the component itself. The receiver is a writable place at the real
    // address, so a handler can also self-locate: `find(#addr_of(this), key)`.
    // No module static, no stored `cp`. (build still runs ONCE and should only
    // read state to produce the tree; the writable receiver is for binding.)
    fn build(ref this) -> Node;
}

// ---- node kinds --------------------------------------------------------

fn kind_label() -> u32 { return 0 as u32; }
fn kind_wrap_label() -> u32 { return 1 as u32; }
fn kind_button() -> u32 { return 2 as u32; }
fn kind_spacer() -> u32 { return 3 as u32; }
fn kind_column() -> u32 { return 4 as u32; }
fn kind_row() -> u32 { return 5 as u32; }
fn kind_text_area() -> u32 { return 6 as u32; }
fn kind_composer() -> u32 { return 7 as u32; }
// kind 8 retired (code_editor, removed 2026-07-15): a code editor is an APP
// widget, not a facet primitive — apps build it natively and embed it through
// the native hatch. The number stays unassigned.
fn kind_bordered() -> u32 { return 9 as u32; }
fn kind_clickable() -> u32 { return 10 as u32; }
fn kind_split() -> u32 { return 11 as u32; }
// Tier-2 leaves (Phase 2) — promoted from the AppKit-only `@ui` layer so the
// portable model covers everything a real app needs.
fn kind_image() -> u32 { return 12 as u32; }
fn kind_symbol() -> u32 { return 13 as u32; }       // SF Symbol / themed icon — PROVISIONAL (no portable meaning yet)
fn kind_text_field() -> u32 { return 14 as u32; }
fn kind_popup() -> u32 { return 15 as u32; }
fn kind_divider() -> u32 { return 16 as u32; }
// The escape hatch (Phase 3): an opaque native view facet does not abstract.
// The backend adopts the handle into a flex payload; the app owns the base view
// (e.g. a component field) and re-wraps it each render. This is the valve that
// carries structured widgets (list/tree/scroll) until a portable kind earns
// its place (contract doc §8).
fn kind_native() -> u32 { return 17 as u32; }
// Overlay container (Phase / matrix §2): children stack. Pure layout — no vtable
// op, handled entirely in `mount` via absolute positioning (like column/row).
fn kind_zstack() -> u32 { return 18 as u32; }
// Form controls (matrix §2) — each a leaf with a vtable op per backend.
fn kind_toggle() -> u32 { return 19 as u32; }
fn kind_slider() -> u32 { return 20 as u32; }
fn kind_progress() -> u32 { return 21 as u32; }
fn kind_stepper() -> u32 { return 22 as u32; }
fn kind_secure_field() -> u32 { return 23 as u32; }
fn kind_segmented() -> u32 { return 24 as u32; }
// Scroll container (matrix §4): a wrapper op — the backend hosts the mounted
// content in a scroll view. Two-phase content sizing is the backend's concern.
fn kind_scroll() -> u32 { return 25 as u32; }
fn kind_color_picker() -> u32 { return 26 as u32; }
fn kind_date_picker() -> u32 { return 27 as u32; }
// Grid container (matrix §1): pure CSS-grid layout — no vtable op (like zstack).
fn kind_grid() -> u32 { return 28 as u32; }
// A plain styled rectangle leaf (Shapes: Rectangle/RoundedRectangle/Circle via
// background + corner_radius). One backend op.
fn kind_box() -> u32 { return 29 as u32; }
// A translucent vibrancy wrapper (macOS material / sidebar). Wrapper op.
fn kind_material() -> u32 { return 30 as u32; }
// A read-only value gauge (SwiftUI Gauge → NSLevelIndicator). Leaf op.
fn kind_gauge() -> u32 { return 31 as u32; }
// A custom vector shape (SwiftUI Path → CAShapeLayer). Leaf op.
fn kind_path() -> u32 { return 32 as u32; }
// window_drag: a wrapper whose backing surface drags the host window (and zooms
// on double-click) — the drag affordance for a custom, hidden-button titlebar.
fn kind_window_drag() -> u32 { return 33 as u32; }
// window_buttons: a native leaf that re-parents the REAL standard window buttons
// (close/miniaturize/zoom) into its host view — the traffic lights for a custom
// titlebar, keeping native behavior (zoom toggle, green-button tiling menu).
fn kind_window_buttons() -> u32 { return 34 as u32; }
// A query field with magnifier + clear button (NSSearchField). Leaf op.
fn kind_search_field() -> u32 { return 35 as u32; }
// An editable dropdown: type a free value or pick a suggestion (NSComboBox).
// Leaf op. Distinct from `popup`, which is a fixed non-editable selector.
fn kind_combo_box() -> u32 { return 36 as u32; }
// An editable field whose text becomes discrete pill tokens (NSTokenField).
// Leaf op.
fn kind_token_field() -> u32 { return 37 as u32; }

// Agent-affordance roles — numeric indices aligned with agent_core::Role so
// Phase 2 can pass them through without a translation table.
fn role_button() -> u32 { return 0 as u32; }
fn role_text() -> u32 { return 1 as u32; }
fn role_input() -> u32 { return 2 as u32; }
fn role_list() -> u32 { return 3 as u32; }
fn role_group() -> u32 { return 4 as u32; }
fn role_window() -> u32 { return 5 as u32; }
// Sentinel: "do NOT record an affordance; pin an explicit id only." Used for the
// native hatch, which re-embeds an ALREADY-mounted widget — its real op already
// declared the right affordance (Input, …); recording a group/none over it every
// re-render would downgrade it to observe-only. Backends set_identity skip the
// affordance write for this role and only apply a non-empty agent_id.
fn role_none() -> u32 { return 255 as u32; }

// Core-owned kind → role table. Used by `mount` when calling `set_identity`.
fn role_for_kind(k: u32) -> u32 {
    if k == kind_button() { return role_button(); }
    if k == kind_clickable() { return role_button(); }
    if k == kind_label() { return role_text(); }
    if k == kind_wrap_label() { return role_text(); }
    if k == kind_text_area() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_composer() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_bordered() { return role_group(); }
    if k == kind_split() { return role_group(); }
    if k == kind_text_field() { return role_input(); }
    // popup holds a readable selection; precise index-drive is a future agent
    // verb (the model has click/text/none) — role_input keeps it observable.
    if k == kind_popup() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_toggle() { return role_button(); }   // click flips it
    if k == kind_slider() { return role_input(); }    // has a readable value
    if k == kind_stepper() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_secure_field() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_segmented() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_color_picker() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_date_picker() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_search_field() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_combo_box() { return role_input(); }
    if k == kind_token_field() { return role_input(); }
    // image / symbol / divider / progress are non-interactive display.
    return role_group();
}

// Default click handler — a no-op, so `Node.click` is never a null fn-pointer.
// (Button always wires a target; optional gesture/composer handlers use the
// null-fn sentinel `0 as fn(*u8,*u8)` instead, so backends know whether to
// install a recognizer at all.)
fn noop(sender: *u8, ctx: *u8) { return; }

// Null-fn sentinel for optional handlers (composer/clickable gestures).
// Installing 11 gesture recognizers unconditionally is waste; backends wire
// only when the field is non-null.
fn null_fn() -> fn(*u8, *u8) { return 0 as fn(*u8, *u8); }

// ---- Style: portable visual style ------------------------------------------
// Every field has an explicit UNSET state so a backend's `apply_style` touches
// ONLY what the author set — it must never stomp a control's size-specific font
// or a dark-mode-adaptive semantic color. Unset = `0.0` (numerics),
// `weight_unset()`/`family_unset()`, a `Color` with token 0 (`Color::none`).

// Font weight tokens (0 = unset — keep the widget's native weight).
fn weight_unset() -> u32 { return 0 as u32; }
fn weight_regular() -> u32 { return 1 as u32; }
fn weight_medium() -> u32 { return 2 as u32; }
fn weight_semibold() -> u32 { return 3 as u32; }
fn weight_bold() -> u32 { return 4 as u32; }

// Font family tokens (0 = unset).
fn family_unset() -> u32 { return 0 as u32; }
fn family_system() -> u32 { return 1 as u32; }
fn family_monospace() -> u32 { return 2 as u32; }

// A color: a semantic token (mapped to the platform's native color) or explicit
// RGBA. token 0 = none/unset; token 255 = Rgba (use r/g/b/a, 0..1). Flat + Copy.
// A color is a NAME (token) or a literal, resolved by the backend at paint
// time. Two tiers of names:
//
//   Tier 1 — the PLATFORM's semantic colors (text tiers, surfaces, fills,
//   selection, separator, accent, the system palette). Pass-through: their
//   job is "look native"; they are never themeable. The escape hatch when
//   the theme roles are not what you mean.
//
//   Tier 2 — THEME roles (primary/secondary brand slots, ink, surface tiers,
//   outline, status). The app retints these with `set_theme`; unset roles
//   fall back to the nearest Tier-1 color, so a themeless app is native in
//   both appearances and a themed app looks the same on every backend.
//
// `rgba` is a fixed literal (identical in light and dark); `adaptive` packs
// a light/dark rgba pair into one Color, resolved by appearance at paint
// time — the primitive behind every Theme slot and the one-off escape for
// app-specific pairs.
struct Color {
    token: u32,
    r: f64,
    g: f64,
    b: f64,
    a: f64,
    // The dark side of an `adaptive` pair (token 254); unused otherwise.
    r2: f64,
    g2: f64,
    b2: f64,
    a2: f64,
}

impl Color {
    // A bare token, all channels zero.
    fn tok(t: u32) -> Color {
        return Color {
            token: t,
            r: 0.0f64, g: 0.0f64, b: 0.0f64, a: 0.0f64,
            r2: 0.0f64, g2: 0.0f64, b2: 0.0f64, a2: 0.0f64,
        };
    }

    fn none() -> Color { return Color::tok(0 as u32); }

    // ---- Tier 1: platform semantic colors -----------------------------------
    // Text tiers.
    fn text() -> Color { return Color::tok(1 as u32); }
    fn text_secondary() -> Color { return Color::tok(2 as u32); }
    fn text_tertiary() -> Color { return Color::tok(9 as u32); }
    fn placeholder() -> Color { return Color::tok(15 as u32); }
    fn link() -> Color { return Color::tok(16 as u32); }
    // The user's chosen system accent.
    fn accent() -> Color { return Color::tok(3 as u32); }
    // Area tiers.
    fn window_background() -> Color { return Color::tok(11 as u32); }
    fn under_page_background() -> Color { return Color::tok(12 as u32); }
    fn control_background() -> Color { return Color::tok(13 as u32); }
    // Neutral fills.
    fn fill() -> Color { return Color::tok(14 as u32); }
    fn fill_secondary() -> Color { return Color::tok(19 as u32); }
    // Selection.
    fn selected_content_background() -> Color { return Color::tok(17 as u32); }
    fn selected_text_background() -> Color { return Color::tok(18 as u32); }
    fn separator() -> Color { return Color::tok(10 as u32); }
    // The system palette.
    fn system_red() -> Color { return Color::tok(4 as u32); }
    fn system_green() -> Color { return Color::tok(5 as u32); }
    fn system_blue() -> Color { return Color::tok(6 as u32); }
    fn system_orange() -> Color { return Color::tok(7 as u32); }
    fn system_gray() -> Color { return Color::tok(8 as u32); }
    fn system_yellow() -> Color { return Color::tok(20 as u32); }
    fn system_purple() -> Color { return Color::tok(21 as u32); }
    fn system_pink() -> Color { return Color::tok(22 as u32); }
    fn system_teal() -> Color { return Color::tok(23 as u32); }
    fn system_indigo() -> Color { return Color::tok(24 as u32); }

    // ---- literals + pairs ---------------------------------------------------
    fn rgba(r: f64, g: f64, b: f64, a: f64) -> Color {
        return Color {
            token: 255 as u32,
            r: r, g: g, b: b, a: a,
            r2: 0.0f64, g2: 0.0f64, b2: 0.0f64, a2: 0.0f64,
        };
    }

    // A light/dark rgba pair in one Color, resolved by the CURRENT appearance
    // at paint time (and re-resolved live on an appearance flip). Pass rgba
    // colors for both sides.
    fn adaptive(light: Color, dark: Color) -> Color {
        return Color {
            token: 254 as u32,
            r: light.r, g: light.g, b: light.b, a: light.a,
            r2: dark.r, g2: dark.g, b2: dark.b, a2: dark.a,
        };
    }

    // ---- Tier 2: theme roles ------------------------------------------------
    // The brand slot: buttons, selection marks, links, badges.
    fn primary() -> Color { return Color::tok(100 as u32); }
    fn on_primary() -> Color { return Color::tok(101 as u32); }
    // The second brand slot.
    fn secondary() -> Color { return Color::tok(102 as u32); }
    fn on_secondary() -> Color { return Color::tok(103 as u32); }
    // The mark base (text / glyph / hairline / translucent fill) at `a` —
    // the same alpha reads identically over both appearances' surfaces.
    fn ink(a: f64 = 1.0f64) -> Color {
        var c: Color = Color::tok(104 as u32);
        c.a = a;
        return c;
    }
    // Surface tiers: the floor, raised chrome (panel / card), and recessed
    // wells.
    fn surface() -> Color { return Color::tok(105 as u32); }
    fn raised() -> Color { return Color::tok(106 as u32); }
    fn sunken() -> Color { return Color::tok(107 as u32); }
    // Extended surface tiers — what IDE-grade chrome needs beyond the three
    // base tiers, each independently retintable by set_theme:
    fn content() -> Color { return Color::tok(112 as u32); }    // editor / document body
    fn toolbar() -> Color { return Color::tok(113 as u32); }    // the chrome row above content
    fn tabstrip() -> Color { return Color::tok(114 as u32); }   // a tab row / strip behind tabs
    fn track() -> Color { return Color::tok(115 as u32); }      // a segmented / switcher track
    fn chip() -> Color { return Color::tok(116 as u32); }       // a discrete raised control chip
    fn recessed() -> Color { return Color::tok(117 as u32); }   // a translucent recessed well
    // Separators and borders.
    fn outline() -> Color { return Color::tok(108 as u32); }
    // Status.
    fn success() -> Color { return Color::tok(109 as u32); }
    fn warning() -> Color { return Color::tok(110 as u32); }
    fn danger() -> Color { return Color::tok(111 as u32); }

    fn is_set(this) -> bool { return this.token != (0 as u32); }
    // Resolution depends on the theme or the appearance (any named token or
    // pair — everything except unset and a fixed rgba literal). What the
    // backend's repaint registry keys on.
    fn is_dynamic(this) -> bool {
        return this.token != (0 as u32) && this.token != (255 as u32);
    }
    fn is_adaptive(this) -> bool { return this.token == (254 as u32); }
    fn is_theme_role(this) -> bool {
        return this.token >= (100 as u32) && this.token <= (117 as u32);
    }
}

// ---- Theme ------------------------------------------------------------------
// The app's palette: one plain record, installed once with `set_theme` (main,
// or App::on_launch). Every field is a Color — unset (the platform default
// chain), a fixed `rgba`, or an `adaptive` light/dark pair. Calling set_theme
// again re-themes the LIVE app: the backend repaints token-colored chrome in
// place, the same sweep an appearance flip runs. No context, no threading —
// a role is spelled `Color::primary()` anywhere, and resolution happens at
// paint time.
struct Theme {
    primary: Color,
    on_primary: Color,
    secondary: Color,
    on_secondary: Color,
    ink: Color,
    surface: Color,
    raised: Color,
    sunken: Color,
    outline: Color,
    success: Color,
    warning: Color,
    danger: Color,
    content: Color,
    toolbar: Color,
    tabstrip: Color,
    track: Color,
    chip: Color,
    recessed: Color,
}

impl Theme {
    fn new(
        primary: Color = Color::none(),
        on_primary: Color = Color::none(),
        secondary: Color = Color::none(),
        on_secondary: Color = Color::none(),
        ink: Color = Color::none(),
        surface: Color = Color::none(),
        raised: Color = Color::none(),
        sunken: Color = Color::none(),
        outline: Color = Color::none(),
        success: Color = Color::none(),
        warning: Color = Color::none(),
        danger: Color = Color::none(),
        content: Color = Color::none(),
        toolbar: Color = Color::none(),
        tabstrip: Color = Color::none(),
        track: Color = Color::none(),
        chip: Color = Color::none(),
        recessed: Color = Color::none(),
    ) -> Theme {
        return Theme {
            primary: primary,
            on_primary: on_primary,
            secondary: secondary,
            on_secondary: on_secondary,
            ink: ink,
            surface: surface,
            raised: raised,
            sunken: sunken,
            outline: outline,
            success: success,
            warning: warning,
            danger: danger,
            content: content,
            toolbar: toolbar,
            tabstrip: tabstrip,
            track: track,
            chip: chip,
            recessed: recessed,
        };
    }
}

static THEME: Theme = #zero::[Theme]();

// Backend hook: repaint token-colored chrome after a theme change (the same
// sweep the appearance observer runs). Installed by the backend; without one
// set_theme still stores (new mounts resolve the new palette).
static THEME_CHANGED_FN: fn() = #zero::[fn()]();
fn set_theme_changed_fn(f: fn()) { THEME_CHANGED_FN = f; return; }

fn set_theme(take t: Theme) {
    THEME = t;
    // Parked outlet siblings bake build-time colors — drop them so the next
    // switch_to re-stages against the new palette (see the outlet section).
    router_theme_invalidate();
    let f: fn() = THEME_CHANGED_FN;
    if f != (0 as fn()) { f(); }
    return;
}

fn theme() -> Theme { return THEME; }

// The stored Color for a theme-role token — none when the app left the role
// unset (resolve `theme_fallback` instead). Backend-facing.
fn theme_slot(token: u32) -> Color {
    if token == (100 as u32) { return THEME.primary; }
    if token == (101 as u32) { return THEME.on_primary; }
    if token == (102 as u32) { return THEME.secondary; }
    if token == (103 as u32) { return THEME.on_secondary; }
    if token == (104 as u32) { return THEME.ink; }
    if token == (105 as u32) { return THEME.surface; }
    if token == (106 as u32) { return THEME.raised; }
    if token == (107 as u32) { return THEME.sunken; }
    if token == (108 as u32) { return THEME.outline; }
    if token == (109 as u32) { return THEME.success; }
    if token == (110 as u32) { return THEME.warning; }
    if token == (111 as u32) { return THEME.danger; }
    if token == (112 as u32) { return THEME.content; }
    if token == (113 as u32) { return THEME.toolbar; }
    if token == (114 as u32) { return THEME.tabstrip; }
    if token == (115 as u32) { return THEME.track; }
    if token == (116 as u32) { return THEME.chip; }
    if token == (117 as u32) { return THEME.recessed; }
    return Color::none();
}

// The Tier-1 default an UNSET theme role resolves to. All fallbacks are
// platform tokens or literals (never another role), so resolution is one
// step deep. Backend-facing.
fn theme_fallback(token: u32) -> Color {
    if token == (100 as u32) { return Color::accent(); }
    if token == (101 as u32) { return Color::rgba(1.0f64, 1.0f64, 1.0f64, 1.0f64); }
    if token == (102 as u32) { return Color::accent(); }
    if token == (103 as u32) { return Color::rgba(1.0f64, 1.0f64, 1.0f64, 1.0f64); }
    if token == (104 as u32) { return Color::text(); }
    if token == (105 as u32) { return Color::window_background(); }
    if token == (106 as u32) { return Color::control_background(); }
    if token == (107 as u32) { return Color::under_page_background(); }
    if token == (108 as u32) { return Color::separator(); }
    if token == (109 as u32) { return Color::system_green(); }
    if token == (110 as u32) { return Color::system_orange(); }
    if token == (111 as u32) { return Color::system_red(); }
    if token == (112 as u32) { return Color::control_background(); }
    if token == (113 as u32) { return Color::window_background(); }
    if token == (114 as u32) { return Color::under_page_background(); }
    if token == (115 as u32) { return Color::fill(); }
    if token == (116 as u32) { return Color::fill_secondary(); }
    if token == (117 as u32) { return Color::fill(); }
    return Color::none();
}

// A widget's visual style. Rides on every `Node`; each backend's `apply_style`
// reads it and applies only the set fields. Paint props (fg/bg/corner/border)
// are the Phase-0 surface; font/weight/family are present but folded in later.
struct Style {
    font_size: f64,        // 0 = unset
    weight: u32,           // 0 = unset
    family: u32,           // 0 = unset
    fg: Color,             // token 0 = unset
    bg: Color,             // token 0 = unset
    corner_radius: f64,    // 0 = unset (also = square, same visual)
    border_width: f64,     // 0 = unset
    border_color: Color,   // token 0 = unset
    opacity: f64,          // -1 = unset (0..1)
    shadow_radius: f64,    // 0 = unset (drop-shadow blur radius)
    line_limit: i32,       // 0 = unset / unlimited (max text lines)
    truncate: bool,        // single-line tail-ellipsis (…) when text overflows
                           // its cell (vs the default mid-glyph clip). A label
                           // property — SwiftUI's implicit `.truncationMode(.tail)`.
    text_align: i32,       // 0 = unset; 1 = leading, 2 = center, 3 = trailing
    hidden: bool,          // invisible but keeps its layout space (vs display:none)
    italic: bool,          // italicize the font
    clip: bool,            // clip content to the (rounded) bounds
    underline: bool,       // underline the text
    strikethrough: bool,   // strike through the text
    grad_from: Color,      // gradient start color (token 0 = no gradient)
    grad_to: Color,        // gradient end color
    grad_dir: GradientDir, // gradient direction (default Vertical, top→bottom)
    rotation: f64,         // rotation in degrees (0 = none)
    scale: f64,            // uniform scale (1.0 = none)
    fade_in: f64,          // entrance fade duration in seconds (0 = none). The
                           // node fades opacity 0→1 when it mounts. SwiftUI's
                           // `.transition(.opacity)` — a one-shot appear animation
                           // that fits the component re-render model (each mount
                           // is a fresh view, so it plays its own entrance).
}

// Text-alignment tokens (0 = unset — keep the widget's natural alignment).
fn text_leading() -> i32 { return 1 as i32; }
fn text_center() -> i32 { return 2 as i32; }
fn text_trailing() -> i32 { return 3 as i32; }

// Gradient direction — a portable start→end for a two-color linear gradient,
// mapped by the backend to its gradient layer's start/end points (SwiftUI
// LinearGradient's start/endPoint). `Vertical` is the default (top→bottom).
enum GradientDir {
    Vertical,       // top → bottom
    Horizontal,     // leading → trailing
    DiagonalTLBR,   // top-left → bottom-right
    DiagonalTRBL,   // top-right → bottom-left
}

fn style_none() -> Style {
    return Style {
        font_size: 0.0f64,
        weight: 0 as u32,
        family: 0 as u32,
        fg: Color::none(),
        bg: Color::none(),
        corner_radius: 0.0f64,
        border_width: 0.0f64,
        border_color: Color::none(),
        opacity: -1.0f64,
        shadow_radius: 0.0f64,
        line_limit: 0 as i32,
        truncate: false,
        text_align: 0 as i32,
        hidden: false,
        italic: false,
        clip: false,
        underline: false,
        strikethrough: false,
        grad_from: Color::none(),
        grad_to: Color::none(),
        grad_dir: GradientDir::Vertical,
        rotation: 0.0f64,
        scale: 1.0f64,
        fade_in: 0.0f64,
    };
}

// ---- the description tree ----------------------------------------------

fn menu_action_quit() -> u32 { return 1 as u32; }
fn menu_action_close_window() -> u32 { return 2 as u32; }
fn menu_action_none() -> u32 { return 0 as u32; }

// Standard first-responder edit commands. These are SEMANTIC (portable) tokens:
// each backend routes them to whatever mechanism delivers the command to the
// focused control — on AppKit the responder-chain selectors undo:/redo:/cut:/
// copy:/paste:/selectAll: (target nil). An app puts these on its Edit menu so
// the keyboard shortcuts work in every text field; a backend that has no
// equivalent leaves them inert (like menu_action_none).
fn menu_action_undo() -> u32 { return 3 as u32; }
fn menu_action_redo() -> u32 { return 4 as u32; }
fn menu_action_cut() -> u32 { return 5 as u32; }
fn menu_action_copy() -> u32 { return 6 as u32; }
fn menu_action_paste() -> u32 { return 7 as u32; }
fn menu_action_select_all() -> u32 { return 8 as u32; }

// The native find bar over the focused text view (Edit > Find …). On AppKit
// these route performTextFinderAction: down the responder chain, with the
// item's tag selecting the operation — the same shape as the edit tokens.
// Backends without an equivalent leave them inert.
fn menu_action_find() -> u32 { return 9 as u32; }
fn menu_action_find_next() -> u32 { return 10 as u32; }
fn menu_action_find_previous() -> u32 { return 11 as u32; }
fn menu_action_find_replace() -> u32 { return 12 as u32; }

struct MenuItem {
    menu: text::Text,
    title: text::Text,
    key: text::Text,
    action: u32,
    // Answered when the MENU OPENS, from the same ctx the click receives, so an
    // item reflects live state without anything being rebuilt — the platform
    // behaviour: inapplicable items grey out instead of disappearing, and the
    // menu keeps a stable shape. Null = always enabled / static title.
    //
    // `title_of` is the half a bool cannot express: `Close "editor_tabs.cplus"`
    // rather than `Close`. Returning an empty Text keeps the built title.
    is_enabled: fn(*u8) -> bool,
    title_of: fn(*u8) -> text::Text,
    // A rule between groups of items rather than an item — every other field is
    // ignored except `menu`, which says which group it divides. Once screens
    // append into shared menus, "where does my group begin" needs an answer.
    is_separator: bool,
    // App-command items: a non-null callback WINS over `action` — the backend
    // wires a target/action trampoline that invokes it (sender = the native
    // menu item, ctx = the bound receiver). null_fn() = token item.
    on_click: fn(*u8, *u8),
    opaque click_ctx: *u8,
}

// The null validation callbacks (an item with neither is always enabled and
// keeps its built title), spelled as fns so call sites never write a cast.
fn null_enabled_fn() -> fn(*u8) -> bool { return 0 as fn(*u8) -> bool; }
fn null_title_fn() -> fn(*u8) -> text::Text { return 0 as fn(*u8) -> text::Text; }

impl MenuItem {
    // Build an item through this rather than a struct literal, so the item can
    // gain fields — a checkmark, a submenu, an image — without breaking every
    // call site (adding `is_enabled` / `title_of` broke every literal there was).
    fn new(
        menu: str,
        title: str,
        key: str = "",
        action: u32 = 2 as u32,                     // menu_action_none()
        on_click: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
        ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
        is_enabled: fn(*u8) -> bool = 0 as fn(*u8) -> bool,
        title_of: fn(*u8) -> text::Text = 0 as fn(*u8) -> text::Text,
        is_separator: bool = false,
    ) -> MenuItem {
        return MenuItem {
            menu: text::from_str(menu),
            title: text::from_str(title),
            key: text::from_str(key),
            action: action,
            is_enabled: is_enabled,
            title_of: title_of,
            is_separator: is_separator,
            on_click: on_click,
            click_ctx: ctx,
        };
    }

    // A dividing rule inside `menu`, at the point it is added.
    fn separator(menu: str) -> MenuItem {
        return MenuItem::new(menu, "", is_separator: true);
    }
}

struct AppMenu {
    title: text::Text,
    items: vec::Vec[MenuItem],
}

fn empty_app_menu() -> AppMenu {
    return AppMenu {
        title: text::new(),
        items: vec::new::[MenuItem](),
    };
}

fn app_menu(title: str) -> AppMenu {
    return AppMenu {
        title: text::from_str(title),
        items: vec::new::[MenuItem](),
    };
}

impl AppMenu {
    fn add_item(ref this, menu: str, title: str, key: str, action: u32) {
        this.items.append(MenuItem::new(menu, title, key: key, action: action));
        return;
    }

    // An app-command item: the menu click runs YOUR code. Bound-method ready —
    // `m.add_command("File", "Save", key: "s", on_click: this.on_save)` — the
    // defaulted ctx takes the receiver.
    // `is_enabled` / `title_of` are answered when the menu opens, from `ctx` —
    // so "Save" greys itself out with no open tab, and an item can name its
    // target (`Close "main.cplus"`), without the menu being rebuilt.
    fn add_command(
        ref this,
        menu: str,
        title: str,
        key: str = "",
        on_click: fn(*u8, *u8) = null_fn(),
        ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
        is_enabled: fn(*u8) -> bool = 0 as fn(*u8) -> bool,
        title_of: fn(*u8) -> text::Text = 0 as fn(*u8) -> text::Text,
    ) {
        this.items.append(MenuItem::new(menu, title, key: key, action: menu_action_none(),
                                        on_click: on_click, ctx: ctx,
                                        is_enabled: is_enabled, title_of: title_of));
        return;
    }

    // Add a dividing rule to `menu` at this point.
    fn add_separator(ref this, menu: str) {
        this.items.append(MenuItem::separator(menu));
        return;
    }

    // Drop every item holding key equivalent `k` (ignoring keyless items).
    fn remove_key(ref this, k: str) {
        if #str_len(k) == (0 as usize) { return; }
        var i: usize = 0 as usize;
        while i < this.items.count() {
            var hit: bool = false;
            match this.items.at_ptr(i) {
                option::Option[*MenuItem]::Some(p) => { hit = { (*p).key.equals(k) }; }
                option::Option[*MenuItem]::None => { }
            }
            if hit {
                let _r: option::Option[MenuItem] = this.items.remove(at: i);
            } else {
                i = i +% (1 as usize);
            }
        }
        return;
    }

    // Merge a screen's own items into this menu.
    //
    // ADDITIVE, not override: the base keeps its items and the screen's are
    // appended to their groups. Override would force every screen to redeclare
    // Edit's whole undo/cut/copy/paste/select-all/find family.
    //
    // On a key-equivalent collision the SCREEN wins — two items cannot share a
    // key equivalent, and the mounted screen is the more specific context. Key
    // uniqueness is therefore required only WITHIN one built menu: `New Project
    // ⌘N` on the launcher and `New File ⌘N` in the workspace never coexist.
    fn extend(ref this, take items: vec::Vec[MenuItem]) {
        var src: vec::Vec[MenuItem] = items;
        loop {
            if src.count() == (0 as usize) { break; }
            match src.remove(at: 0 as usize) {
                option::Option[MenuItem]::Some(it) => {
                    let k: text::Text = it.key.clone();
                    this.remove_key(k.view());
                    this.items.append(it);
                }
                option::Option[MenuItem]::None => { break; }
            }
        }
        return;
    }
}

// One node. Widget config is read by the platform's per-kind ops; the
// `_`-prefixed layout props are applied by `mount` and never cross the
// package boundary. Config is a flat union-of-fields (closed kind set —
// no generic prop channel). Per-node memory is irrelevant at UI scale.
struct Node {
    kind: u32,
    text: text::Text,
    click: fn(*u8, *u8),    // (sender, ctx) — wired by the backend's button op
    opaque click_ctx: *u8,  // borrowed identity value; the app owns it
    opaque item: *u8,       // 0 = none. A SECOND app-owned pointer (the bound
                            // method's `this` occupies click_ctx), recovered in a
                            // handler via `item_of(sender)` — per-item data
                            // without encoding it into the key. The app owns it.
    agent_id: text::Text,   // "" = untagged. A developer-curated stable id the
                            // backend pins on the created widget at every mount
                            // (via set_identity), so it survives re-renders.
    tooltip: text::Text,    // "" = none. Hover help text (backend applies in apply_style).
    a11y_label: text::Text, // "" = none. VoiceOver / accessibility label.
    a11y_hint: text::Text,  // "" = none. VoiceOver hint (SwiftUI .accessibilityHint).
    key_equiv: text::Text,  // "" = none. Keyboard shortcut key for a button.
    ctx_menu_items: vec::Vec[text::Text],   // right-click menu items (empty = none)
    ctx_menu_action: fn(*u8, *u8),          // fires (item, index) on a menu pick
    font_size: f64,         // 0 = platform default
    bold: bool,
    secondary: bool,
    primary: bool,
    plain: bool,            // button: borderless/plain variant (link/plain button fold)
    style: Style,           // portable visual style; backend applies set fields
    // --- kind-specific config (union-of-fields; unused kinds leave defaults) ---
    value: text::Text,          // composer / text_area initial text; image path; symbol name
    placeholder: text::Text,    // text_area / text_field
    popup_items: vec::Vec[text::Text],  // popup / segmented: the rows/segments
    popup_selected: i64,        // popup / segmented: initially-selected index
    opaque native_handle: *u8,  // native escape hatch: a borrowed native view; the app owns it
    bool_value: bool,           // toggle: on/off state
    value_f: f64,               // slider / stepper / progress: current value
    min_val: f64,               // slider / stepper: minimum (default 0)
    max_val: f64,               // slider / stepper: maximum (default 1)
    step_val: f64,              // stepper: increment (default 1)
    indeterminate: bool,        // progress: spinner mode (ignore value)
    pull_down: bool,            // popup: pull-down menu mode (Menu) vs selector
    toggle_style: i32,          // toggle: 0 = switch, 1 = checkbox, 2 = radio
    grid_cols: i32,             // grid: number of equal columns
    editable: bool,             // text_area (default true)
    soft_wrap: bool,            // text_area: false = no soft wrap + horizontal scroll (code)
    smart_subs: bool,           // text_area: false = no smart quotes/dashes/replacement (code)
    line_numbers: bool,         // text_area: true = a line-number gutter (code)
    show_border: bool,          // composer (default true)
    draws_background: bool,     // composer (default true)
    radius: f64,                // bordered corner radius
    vertical: bool,             // split: true = side-by-side
    position: f64,              // split: initial divider offset
    min_first: f64,             // split: first-pane minimum
    min_second: f64,            // split: second-pane minimum
    inset_w: f64,               // text_area text-container inset
    inset_h: f64,
    long_press_seconds: f64,    // clickable long-press threshold (0 = platform default)
    // Optional handlers — null-fn sentinel when unset (see null_fn).
    // Clickable's primary click reuses `click` (avoids shadowing the fluent
    // `.on_click` method); remaining gestures are null-fn when unset.
    on_submit: fn(*u8, *u8),    // composer
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8),    // composer / text_area
    on_double_click: fn(*u8, *u8),
    on_right_click: fn(*u8, *u8),
    on_long_press: fn(*u8, *u8),
    on_drag: fn(*u8, *u8),
    on_pinch: fn(*u8, *u8),
    on_rotate: fn(*u8, *u8),
    on_hover_in: fn(*u8, *u8),
    on_hover_out: fn(*u8, *u8),
    on_scroll: fn(*u8, *u8),
    // Raw key input while this element has focus (see key_code/key_chars).
    // Named `key_handler`, not `on_key`, so the fluent `.on_key()` method is
    // not shadowed by a same-named field — the same reason `on_drop`'s field is
    // called `drop_handler`.
    key_handler: fn(*u8, *u8),
    opaque key_ctx: *u8,
    // Drag-and-drop DESTINATION (distinct from the on_drag pan gesture above):
    // when set on a `box`, the box accepts a text drop and this fires with the
    // drop-zone view as sender — read the payload via `dropped_text(sender)`.
    // Field is `drop_handler` (not `on_drop`) so the fluent `.on_drop()` method
    // isn't shadowed by a same-named field.
    drop_handler: fn(*u8, *u8), // null-fn when the box is not a drop target
    opaque drop_ctx: *u8,       // borrowed identity value passed to the handler
    // Drag-over feedback: ONE handler, called on entry AND on the way out, with
    // the current state readable from the sender (`drag_targeted`). Separate
    // from drop_handler because a node may want one without the other — a lane
    // that only highlights, or a drop target with no visual response.
    drag_target_handler: fn(*u8, *u8),
    opaque drag_over_ctx: *u8,
    drag_text: text::Text,      // drag-and-drop SOURCE payload; "" = not draggable
    // layout
    _grow: f64,
    _gap: f64,
    _padding: f64,
    _width: f64,            // -1 = auto
    _height: f64,           // -1 = auto
    // extended flexbox layout — thin pass-through to flex_layout (the shared
    // engine); mount replays each set field onto the flex node. Uses flex's own
    // CSS vocabulary + enums (not SwiftUI). Unset = a `_has_*` flag (enums) or a
    // -1 sentinel (sizes, where 0 is a valid value). Per-edge padding composes
    // OVER the uniform `_padding` (edges win).
    _align_items: flex::Align,   _has_align_items: bool,
    _align_self: flex::Align,    _has_align_self: bool,
    _justify: flex::Justify,     _has_justify: bool,
    _shrink: f64,           // -1 = unset (0 is valid)
    _min_w: f64,            // -1 = unset
    _min_h: f64,            // -1 = unset
    _max_w: f64,            // -1 = unset
    _max_h: f64,            // -1 = unset
    _basis: f64,            // -1 = unset (flex-basis, points)
    _aspect: f64,           // -1 = unset (width/height)
    _wrap: flex::Wrap,           _has_wrap: bool,
    _position_type: flex::PositionType, _has_position_type: bool,   // absolute → ZStack/overlay
    _pad_l: f64, _pad_t: f64, _pad_r: f64, _pad_b: f64,   // 0 = unset (per-edge padding)
    _mrg_l: f64, _mrg_t: f64, _mrg_r: f64, _mrg_b: f64,   // 0 = unset (per-edge margin)
    _ins_l: f64, _ins_t: f64, _ins_r: f64, _ins_b: f64,   // absolute inset values
    _inset_mask: u32,       // bit per set inset edge: 1=L 2=T 4=R 8=B (0 is a valid inset)
    _width_pct: f64,        // -1 = unset (percent width, overrides points)
    _height_pct: f64,       // -1 = unset (percent height)
    _gap_row: f64,          // 0 = unset (vertical gutter)
    _gap_col: f64,          // 0 = unset (horizontal gutter)
    _display: flex::Display,       _has_display: bool,
    _z_index: i32,                 _has_z_index: bool,
    _overflow: flex::Overflow,     _has_overflow: bool,
    _align_content: flex::Align,   _has_align_content: bool,
    _direction: flex::Direction,   _has_direction: bool,
    _box_sizing: flex::BoxSizing,  _has_box_sizing: bool,
    _flex_dir: flex::FlexDirection, _has_flex_dir: bool,   // override kind dir (reverse)
    _grid_col_span: i32,           // 0 = unset (grid child)
    _grid_row_span: i32,           // 0 = unset
    _grid_col_start: i32,          // 0 = unset (1-based line)
    _grid_row_start: i32,          // 0 = unset
    _grid_cols_subgrid: bool,      // grid: inherit parent's column tracks
    _grid_rows_subgrid: bool,      // grid: inherit parent's row tracks
    _bdr_l: f64, _bdr_t: f64, _bdr_r: f64, _bdr_b: f64,   // 0 = unset (border-as-inset)
    _pad_start: f64, _pad_end: f64,   // 0 = unset (logical, direction-relative padding)
    _mrg_start: f64, _mrg_end: f64,   // 0 = unset (logical margin)
    path_cmds: vec::Vec[PathCmd],     // path kind: the vector segments (empty = none)
    toolbar_items: vec::Vec[Node],    // window toolbar items (buttons); empty = no toolbar
    children: vec::Vec[Node],
}

// One segment of a vector `path`. `op` selects which points are read:
// 1 move / 2 line use (ax,ay); 3 quad adds control (bx,by); 4 cubic adds a
// second control (cx,cy); 5 close ignores all points. Local top-left coords.
struct PathCmd {
    op: u32,
    ax: f64, ay: f64,   // end point
    bx: f64, by: f64,   // control 1 (quad control; cubic control 1)
    cx: f64, cy: f64,   // control 2 (cubic only)
}

fn path_op_move() -> u32 { return 1 as u32; }
fn path_op_line() -> u32 { return 2 as u32; }
fn path_op_quad() -> u32 { return 3 as u32; }
fn path_op_cubic() -> u32 { return 4 as u32; }
fn path_op_close() -> u32 { return 5 as u32; }

fn blank(kind: u32) -> Node {
    let none: fn(*u8, *u8) = null_fn();
    return Node {
        kind: kind,
        text: text::from_str(""),
        click: noop,
        click_ctx: 0 as *u8,
        item: 0 as *u8,
        agent_id: text::from_str(""),
        tooltip: text::from_str(""),
        a11y_label: text::from_str(""),
        a11y_hint: text::from_str(""),
        key_equiv: text::from_str(""),
        ctx_menu_items: vec::new::[text::Text](),
        ctx_menu_action: null_fn(),
        font_size: 0.0f64,
        bold: false,
        secondary: false,
        primary: false,
        plain: false,
        style: style_none(),
        value: text::from_str(""),
        placeholder: text::from_str(""),
        popup_items: vec::new::[text::Text](),
        popup_selected: 0 as i64,
        native_handle: 0 as *u8,
        bool_value: false,
        value_f: 0.0f64,
        min_val: 0.0f64,
        max_val: 1.0f64,
        step_val: 1.0f64,
        indeterminate: false,
        pull_down: false,
        toggle_style: 0 as i32,
        grid_cols: 0 as i32,
        editable: true,
        soft_wrap: true,
        smart_subs: true,
        line_numbers: false,
        show_border: true,
        draws_background: true,
        radius: 6.0f64,
        vertical: true,
        position: 220.0f64,
        min_first: 150.0f64,
        min_second: 240.0f64,
        inset_w: 0.0f64,
        inset_h: 0.0f64,
        long_press_seconds: 0.0f64,
        on_submit: none,
        on_change: none,
        on_double_click: none,
        on_right_click: none,
        on_long_press: none,
        on_drag: none,
        on_pinch: none,
        on_rotate: none,
        on_hover_in: none,
        on_hover_out: none,
        on_scroll: none,
        key_handler: none,
        key_ctx: 0 as *u8,
        drop_handler: none,
        drop_ctx: 0 as *u8,
        drag_target_handler: none,
        drag_over_ctx: 0 as *u8,
        drag_text: text::from_str(""),
        _grow: 0.0f64,
        _gap: 0.0f64,
        _padding: 0.0f64,
        _width: -1.0f64,
        _height: -1.0f64,
        _align_items: flex::Align::Auto,    _has_align_items: false,
        _align_self: flex::Align::Auto,     _has_align_self: false,
        _justify: flex::Justify::FlexStart, _has_justify: false,
        _shrink: -1.0f64,
        _min_w: -1.0f64,
        _min_h: -1.0f64,
        _max_w: -1.0f64,
        _max_h: -1.0f64,
        _basis: -1.0f64,
        _aspect: -1.0f64,
        _wrap: flex::Wrap::NoWrap,               _has_wrap: false,
        _position_type: flex::PositionType::Static, _has_position_type: false,
        _pad_l: 0.0f64, _pad_t: 0.0f64, _pad_r: 0.0f64, _pad_b: 0.0f64,
        _mrg_l: 0.0f64, _mrg_t: 0.0f64, _mrg_r: 0.0f64, _mrg_b: 0.0f64,
        _ins_l: 0.0f64, _ins_t: 0.0f64, _ins_r: 0.0f64, _ins_b: 0.0f64,
        _inset_mask: 0 as u32,
        _width_pct: -1.0f64,
        _height_pct: -1.0f64,
        _gap_row: 0.0f64,
        _gap_col: 0.0f64,
        _display: flex::Display::Flex,          _has_display: false,
        _z_index: 0 as i32,                     _has_z_index: false,
        _overflow: flex::Overflow::Visible,     _has_overflow: false,
        _align_content: flex::Align::Auto,      _has_align_content: false,
        _direction: flex::Direction::Inherit,   _has_direction: false,
        _box_sizing: flex::BoxSizing::BorderBox, _has_box_sizing: false,
        _flex_dir: flex::FlexDirection::Column,  _has_flex_dir: false,
        _grid_col_span: 0 as i32,
        _grid_row_span: 0 as i32,
        _grid_col_start: 0 as i32,
        _grid_row_start: 0 as i32,
        _grid_cols_subgrid: false,
        _grid_rows_subgrid: false,
        _bdr_l: 0.0f64, _bdr_t: 0.0f64, _bdr_r: 0.0f64, _bdr_b: 0.0f64,
        _pad_start: 0.0f64, _pad_end: 0.0f64,
        _mrg_start: 0.0f64, _mrg_end: 0.0f64,
        path_cmds: vec::new::[PathCmd](),
        toolbar_items: vec::new::[Node](),
        children: vec::new::[Node](),
    };
}

// ---- leaves --------------------------------------------------------------

fn label(s: str, size: f64 = 0.0f64, bold: bool = false, secondary: bool = false) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_label());
    n.text = text::from_str(s);
    n.font_size = size;
    n.bold = bold;
    n.secondary = secondary;
    return n;
}

// A multi-line label that wraps to its flexed width.
fn wrap_label(s: str, size: f64 = 0.0f64, secondary: bool = false) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_wrap_label());
    n.text = text::from_str(s);
    n.font_size = size;
    n.secondary = secondary;
    return n;
}

fn button(s: str, primary: bool = false) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_button());
    n.text = text::from_str(s);
    n.primary = primary;
    return n;
}

// An icon-only push button: a borderless button showing the named SF Symbol
// (SwiftUI `Button { } label: { Image(systemName:) }`). The symbol name rides
// in `value` (the field already doubles as an image/symbol name); ak_button
// branches to an image-only button when it is set. Wire it with `.on_click`.
fn icon_button(symbol_name: str) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_button());
    n.value = text::from_str(symbol_name);
    return n;
}

// Flexible empty space.
fn spacer() -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_spacer());
    n._grow = 1.0f64;
    return n;
}

// Multi-line editable text area. Optional `on_change` uses the null-fn sentinel
// when unset (same convention as composer).
//
// The code-editing knobs (a code editor is this text_area configured, not a
// separate kind): `wrap: false` disables soft wrap and adds a horizontal
// scroller; `smart_substitutions: false` turns off the code-hostile automatic
// quotes/dashes/text replacement; `line_numbers: true` adds a line-number
// gutter. Font and color ride the Style channels — `.monospaced()`,
// `.font(size)`, `.foreground_color(...)` reach the inner text view (the caret
// follows the foreground). An editable area supports undo.
fn text_area(
    value: str = "",
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
    editable: bool = true,
    show_border: bool = true,
    draws_background: bool = true,
    inset_w: f64 = 0.0f64,
    inset_h: f64 = 0.0f64,
    wrap: bool = true,
    smart_substitutions: bool = true,
    line_numbers: bool = false,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_text_area());
    n.value = text::from_str(value);
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    n.editable = editable;   // `false` = a read-only scrolling text view
    // Chrome (composer precedent): drop the bezel, clear the fill,
    // pad the text container — a borderless transparent transcript with inset.
    n.show_border = show_border;
    n.draws_background = draws_background;
    n.inset_w = inset_w;
    n.inset_h = inset_h;
    n.soft_wrap = wrap;
    n.smart_subs = smart_substitutions;
    n.line_numbers = line_numbers;
    return n;
}

// Chat-style composer: submit on Enter, newline on Shift+Enter or Ctrl+Enter.
// Optional handlers use the null-fn sentinel when unset. `ctx` sits RIGHT
// AFTER `on_submit` so a component can bind an instance method — the
// bound-reference rule (E0824) requires the defaulted `*u8` context parameter
// adjacent to the handler it fills: `composer(on_submit: this.send, ...)`.
fn composer(
    on_submit: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    value: str = "",
    show_border: bool = true,
    draws_background: bool = true,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_composer());
    n.on_submit = on_submit;
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    n.value = text::from_str(value);
    n.show_border = show_border;
    n.draws_background = draws_background;
    return n;
}

// ---- Tier-2 leaves (Phase 2) -----------------------------------------------
// Promoted from the AppKit-only `@ui` layer. Each is a portable kind + op; the
// backend adapts its existing widget. `colored_label` folds into `label` +
// `.foreground_color`, and `icon_button`/`link_button`/`plain_button` fold into
// `button` + `.plain()`/`.foreground_color` or `clickable { symbol(...) }`.

// A bitmap image loaded from a file path.
fn image(path: str) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_image());
    n.value = text::from_str(path);
    return n;
}

// An SF Symbol / themed icon by name. PROVISIONAL — SF Symbols are an Apple-ism
// with no portable meaning yet; a non-Apple backend maps a themed icon set.
fn symbol(name: str, size: f64 = 16.0f64, secondary: bool = false) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_symbol());
    n.value = text::from_str(name);
    n.font_size = size;
    n.secondary = secondary;
    return n;
}

// A single-line editable text field (agent-drivable via set_text).
fn text_field(placeholder: str = "", value: str = "") -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_text_field());
    n.placeholder = text::from_str(placeholder);
    n.value = text::from_str(value);
    return n;
}

// A search field (NSSearchField): a query box with a magnifier and a clear
// button. Editable like `text_field` — read the query with the backend field
// reader. `on_search` fires when the user commits (Return / send delay).
fn search_field(
    placeholder: str = "",
    value: str = "",
    on_search: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_search_field());
    n.placeholder = text::from_str(placeholder);
    n.value = text::from_str(value);
    n.on_change = on_search;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A dropdown selector. `items` are the rows; `selected` the initial index.
// `on_change` fires on a new selection (read the index with the backend reader).
fn popup(
    take items: vec::Vec[text::Text],
    selected: i64 = 0 as i64,
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_popup());
    n.popup_items = items;
    n.popup_selected = selected;
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A pull-down actions menu (SwiftUI `Menu`): a fixed `title` button that drops a
// list of action `items`; `on_change` fires with the chosen item. Same kind as
// `popup` (a pull-down NSPopUpButton) — a variant, not a new contract slot.
fn menu(
    title: str,
    take items: vec::Vec[text::Text],
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_popup());
    n.text = text::from_str(title);
    n.popup_items = items;
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    n.pull_down = true;
    return n;
}

// An editable dropdown (NSComboBox): the user types a free value OR picks one
// of `items`. `value` is the initial text; `on_change` fires on selection or
// commit. Read the current text with the backend field reader — unlike `popup`,
// the value is not restricted to the listed rows.
fn combo_box(
    take items: vec::Vec[text::Text],
    value: str = "",
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_combo_box());
    n.popup_items = items;
    n.value = text::from_str(value);
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A token field (NSTokenField): free text that becomes discrete pill tokens
// (tags, recipients). `tokens` seed it; `on_change` fires on edit. Read the
// comma-joined tokens with the backend field reader (split on ',').
fn token_field(
    take tokens: vec::Vec[text::Text],
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_token_field());
    n.popup_items = tokens;
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A thin separator line.
fn divider() -> Node {
    return blank(kind_divider());
}

// A styled rectangle (Shapes). Paint it with `.background(...)`; round it with
// `.corner_radius(...)` (a full-radius square is a circle). Size with `.frame`.
fn box() -> Node {
    return blank(kind_box());
}

// A CSS-grid container: `columns` equal tracks; children auto-flow into cells,
// wrapping to new rows. Pure layout (like vstack/zstack) — no backend op.
fn grid(take b: Builder, columns: i32 = 2) -> Node {
    let Builder { children } = b;
    var n: Node = blank(kind_grid());
    n.children = children;
    n.grid_cols = columns;
    return n;
}

// A boolean on/off switch. `on` is the initial state; `on_change` fires when the
// user flips it (read the new state in the handler).
fn toggle(
    on: bool = false,
    label: str = "",
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_toggle());
    n.bool_value = on;
    n.text = text::from_str(label);
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A checkbox (a labeled on/off tick) — a toggle rendered as a checkbox.
fn checkbox(
    label: str = "",
    on: bool = false,
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_toggle());
    n.toggle_style = 1 as i32;
    n.bool_value = on;
    n.text = text::from_str(label);
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A radio button (one of a mutually-exclusive set — grouping is the app's job).
fn radio(
    label: str = "",
    on: bool = false,
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_toggle());
    n.toggle_style = 2 as i32;
    n.bool_value = on;
    n.text = text::from_str(label);
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A color well (opens the system color picker).
fn color_picker(on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8), ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_color_picker());
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A date picker (defaults to the current date).
fn date_picker(on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8), ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_date_picker());
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A continuous value slider over [min, max].
fn slider(
    value: f64 = 0.0f64,
    min: f64 = 0.0f64,
    max: f64 = 1.0f64,
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_slider());
    n.value_f = value;
    n.min_val = min;
    n.max_val = max;
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A progress indicator. `value` in [0,1] when determinate; `indeterminate: true`
// spins (barber-pole / activity) and ignores value.
fn progress(value: f64 = 0.0f64, indeterminate: bool = false) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_progress());
    n.value_f = value;
    n.indeterminate = indeterminate;
    return n;
}

// A read-only value gauge over [min, max] (SwiftUI `Gauge(value:in:)`).
fn gauge(value: f64 = 0.0f64, min: f64 = 0.0f64, max: f64 = 1.0f64) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_gauge());
    n.value_f = value;
    n.min_val = min;
    n.max_val = max;
    return n;
}

// A custom vector shape drawn into a width×height box (SwiftUI `Path`). Build
// the outline with `.move_to`/`.line_to`/`.quad_to`/`.curve_to`/`.close_path`,
// then fill with `.background(color)` and/or stroke with `.border(w, color)`.
// Coordinates are local, top-left origin.
fn path(width: f64, height: f64) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_path());
    n._width = width;
    n._height = height;
    return n;
}

// Read the fixed layout box (backends need it to size a path's drawing surface;
// `_width`/`_height` are module-private, so expose them through the package). -1
// means auto/unset.
fn fixed_width(np: *Node) -> f64 { return { (*np)._width }; }
fn fixed_height(np: *Node) -> f64 { return { (*np)._height }; }

// A +/- numeric stepper over [min, max] by `step`.
fn stepper(
    value: f64 = 0.0f64,
    min: f64 = 0.0f64,
    max: f64 = 100.0f64,
    step: f64 = 1.0f64,
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_stepper());
    n.value_f = value;
    n.min_val = min;
    n.max_val = max;
    n.step_val = step;
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// A masked single-line field for passwords/secrets.
fn secure_field(placeholder: str = "", value: str = "") -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_secure_field());
    n.placeholder = text::from_str(placeholder);
    n.value = text::from_str(value);
    return n;
}

// A horizontal segmented selector (mutually-exclusive segments).
fn segmented(
    take items: vec::Vec[text::Text],
    selected: i64 = 0 as i64,
    on_change: fn(*u8, *u8) = 0 as fn(*u8, *u8),
    ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8,
) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_segmented());
    n.popup_items = items;
    n.popup_selected = selected;
    n.on_change = on_change;
    n.click_ctx = ctx;
    return n;
}

// The native escape hatch: place a platform view facet does not abstract into a
// `@facet` tree. `handle` is a borrowed native view pointer; the app owns the
// underlying view (typically a component field) and produces this node fresh on
// each render (the backend adopts a retained reference into the flex payload).
// Size it with `.frame(w,h)` / `.grow(...)` — facet does not measure an opaque
// view. Platform code that BUILDS handles lives in per-OS files (§5b) — this
// core constructor only carries the pointer.
fn native(handle: *u8) -> Node {
    var n: Node = blank(kind_native());
    n.native_handle = handle;
    return n;
}

// ---- list: a recycling list, backed by the platform's native list widget ----
// A long, scrollable list (chat transcripts, logs, feeds) where only the
// on-screen rows are built. `count` rows total; `row(i, ctx)` builds row i's
// description on demand. Because it is called LAZILY — as rows scroll into view,
// after `render` returns — the row builder's data must OUTLIVE the render. The
// idiomatic form is a bound method reference on a long-lived model:
//
//     facet::list(count: MODEL.len(), row: MODEL.render_row)
//
// `MODEL.render_row` (a `fn render_row(this, i: i64) -> Node` method) auto-fills
// `ctx` with `#addr_of(MODEL)`, so the row builder is a plain method reading its
// own state — no free function, no manual ctx threading. (`ctx` is the trailing
// param because that is where a bound method reference lands the receiver.) A
// plain `fn(i64, *u8)` + explicit `ctx` also works for non-method builders.
//
// The heavy lifting (view reuse, height measurement) is the backend's native
// list; facet stays neutral by dispatching through a builder the backend
// installs at startup (`set_list_builder`). Until one is installed, `list` is an
// empty node — the same portable-by-default posture as any unimplemented op.
static LIST_BUILDER: fn(i64, fn(i64, *u8) -> Node, *u8) -> Node = #zero::[fn(i64, fn(i64, *u8) -> Node, *u8) -> Node]();

// Install the backend's native recycling-list builder (called once at startup,
// before the first render). See facet_appkit `install_list`.
fn set_list_builder(f: fn(i64, fn(i64, *u8) -> Node, *u8) -> Node) {
    LIST_BUILDER = f;
    return;
}

fn list(count: i64, row: fn(i64, *u8) -> Node, ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8) -> Node {
    let f: fn(i64, fn(i64, *u8) -> Node, *u8) -> Node = LIST_BUILDER;
    if f == (0 as fn(i64, fn(i64, *u8) -> Node, *u8) -> Node) { return box(); }
    return f(count, row, ctx);
}

// ---- raise: the by-key update verb (bring a keyed element to the front) ------
// facet is not reactive: to switch which of several overlapping elements shows,
// you don't rebuild — you address the one tagged `key` and bring it to the front
// of its siblings, in place. `raise(sender, key)` is that verb; `sender` is the
// event context (the control a handler was invoked from) the backend navigates
// from to reach the live tree. Backend-installed, like `list`.
static RAISE_FN: fn(*u8, str) = #zero::[fn(*u8, str)]();

fn set_raise_fn(f: fn(*u8, str)) { RAISE_FN = f; return; }

fn raise(sender: *u8, key: str) {
    let f: fn(*u8, str) = RAISE_FN;
    if f == (0 as fn(*u8, str)) { return; }
    f(sender, key);
    return;
}

// ---- key_of: read a sender's key back (which item fired?) --------------------
// The inverse of tagging: a per-item handler bound as an instance method spends
// its ctx on `this`, so it recovers WHICH item fired from `sender`. `key_of`
// returns the sender's key (its agent id) as an owned Text — empty if the sender
// (and, in the AppKit backend, every ancestor view above it) is untagged. With
// namespaced keys ("tabs:tab:<hash>"), the handler parses the id segment out and
// looks the item up. Backend-installed, like `raise`; the backend also
// normalizes gesture-recognizer senders to the view they are attached to.
static KEY_OF_FN: fn(*u8) -> text::Text = #zero::[fn(*u8) -> text::Text]();

fn set_key_of_fn(f: fn(*u8) -> text::Text) { KEY_OF_FN = f; return; }

fn key_of(sender: *u8) -> text::Text {
    let f: fn(*u8) -> text::Text = KEY_OF_FN;
    if f == (0 as fn(*u8) -> text::Text) { return text::new(); }
    return f(sender);
}

// The per-node app pointer set by `.item(p)`, recovered in a handler from the
// sender — the backend walks up to the nearest node that carries one (exact
// parallel to `key_of`). 0 when unset or no backend. The app owns the pointee.
static ITEM_OF_FN: fn(*u8) -> *u8 = #zero::[fn(*u8) -> *u8]();

fn set_item_of_fn(f: fn(*u8) -> *u8) { ITEM_OF_FN = f; return; }

fn item_of(sender: *u8) -> *u8 {
    let f: fn(*u8) -> *u8 = ITEM_OF_FN;
    if f == (0 as fn(*u8) -> *u8) { return 0 as *u8; }
    return f(sender);
}

// ---- dropped_text: read a drop's payload (what landed?) ----------------------
// An `.on_drop` handler's sender is the drop-zone view; `dropped_text(sender)`
// returns the text the drag carried as an owned Text (empty when nothing has
// been dropped). Pair with `key_of(sender)` for WHERE it landed. Backend-
// installed, like `key_of`.
static DROPPED_TEXT_FN: fn(*u8) -> text::Text = #zero::[fn(*u8) -> text::Text]();

fn set_dropped_text_fn(f: fn(*u8) -> text::Text) { DROPPED_TEXT_FN = f; return; }

fn dropped_text(sender: *u8) -> text::Text {
    let f: fn(*u8) -> text::Text = DROPPED_TEXT_FN;
    if f == (0 as fn(*u8) -> text::Text) { return text::new(); }
    return f(sender);
}

// ---- is_dark: which system appearance is active ------------------------------
// True when the platform is in its dark appearance. An app choosing custom
// colors branches on this at build/re-theme time; prefer the adaptive Color
// tokens where one fits (they track the appearance without any branching).
// Backend-installed; false until a backend registers (light is the neutral
// default for headless/portable runs).
static IS_DARK_FN: fn() -> bool = #zero::[fn() -> bool]();

fn set_is_dark_fn(f: fn() -> bool) { IS_DARK_FN = f; return; }

fn is_dark() -> bool {
    let f: fn() -> bool = IS_DARK_FN;
    if f == (0 as fn() -> bool) { return false; }
    return f();
}

// ---- appearance change: the re-theme signal ----------------------------------
// The backend fires `notify_appearance_changed()` on every system light/dark
// flip (macOS: viewDidChangeEffectiveAppearance). The app registers ONE handler
// with `on_appearance_change`; what it does — re-theme keyed elements in place,
// swap a root outlet's child — is app policy, never facet's. Colors flattened
// at build time (layer backgrounds) do not track the appearance by themselves,
// so a themed app re-applies them from this handler.
static APPEARANCE_CB: fn(*u8) = #zero::[fn(*u8)]();
static APPEARANCE_CB_CTX: *u8 = #zero::[*u8]();

fn on_appearance_change(handler: fn(*u8), ctx: *u8 = 0 as *u8) {
    APPEARANCE_CB = handler;
    APPEARANCE_CB_CTX = ctx;
    return;
}

// Backend entry: deliver the flip to the app's registered handler (no-op when
// the app never registered).
fn notify_appearance_changed() {
    let f: fn(*u8) = APPEARANCE_CB;
    if f == (0 as fn(*u8)) { return; }
    f(APPEARANCE_CB_CTX);
    return;
}

// ---- find: the keyed-direct update path (address one element, mutate it) -----
// This is how facet updates. facet is NOT reactive: a handler mutates a struct
// field, then pushes that value to the ONE element that shows it — addressed by
// its `key`, edited in place. There is no re-render and no diff; the app already
// holds the key, so there is no delta to discover. (Contrast: React mutates data,
// re-renders, then diffs a virtual tree to rediscover the change.)
//
//     fn increment(sender: *u8, ctx: *u8) {
//         COUNTER.n = COUNTER.n + 1;
//         facet::find(ctx, "value").set_text("${COUNTER.n}");   // in place, done
//     }
//
// `cp` is the component's own address — a handler receives it as its `ctx`, so
// `find(ctx, key)` resolves `key` inside THAT component's live subtree (keys are
// component-scoped, not a global namespace). A miss yields an EMPTY handle whose
// mutators no-op — the `getElementById` null-object pattern (facet has no null),
// so a `find` on a torn-down element is safe. Backend-installed, like `list`.
//
// A `Handle` is a NON-OWNING view onto a mounted element: it sets content, it
// never moves or drops the node (that is `take`). Its fields are opaque plumbing
// the backend fills; apps only call the mutators. It is chainable and Copy.
struct Handle {
    opaque _view: *u8,     // located native view (0 = not found), owned by its superview
    opaque _flex: *u8,     // retained layout node (re-measure / relayout), owned by the tree
    opaque _cp: *u8,       // owning component (relayout scope)
    _kind: u32,            // reserved: node kind for setter dispatch
}

// Backend hooks, installed once at startup (see facet_appkit `install`). Until a
// backend registers them, `find` returns a missing handle and mutators no-op —
// the same portable-by-default posture as `list` / `raise`.
static FIND_FN: fn(*u8, str) -> Handle = #zero::[fn(*u8, str) -> Handle]();
static SET_TEXT_FN: fn(Handle, str) -> bool = #zero::[fn(Handle, str) -> bool]();
